; cow-maintenance.service — managed by database/cron/install-maintenance-timer.sh
; (do not edit the installed copy in ~/.config/systemd/user; edit THIS template,
; commit, and redeploy).
;
; __REPO_DIR__ is substituted with the absolute repo path at install time, so the
; unit never depends on the checkout living at any particular path.
;
; PPE ONLY. Dev is intentionally left with no schedule — run the SQL by hand with
; `psql -f` when testing. See docs/STREAK_EXPIRATION_CRON.md.

[Unit]
Description=Vocabulary app hourly maintenance (inactivity penalty + study-challenge expiry)
Documentation=file://__REPO_DIR__/docs/STREAK_EXPIRATION_CRON.md
; Nothing to order against: the jobs talk to Docker containers, not host services,
; and both tolerate the container being down (they fail, log, and retry next hour).

[Service]
Type=oneshot

;The steps run STRICTLY IN SEQUENCE by systemd (ExecStart lines in one oneshot unit).
; (Until 2026-11-09 a middle step pruned dangling Night Market templates; it was removed
; with the feature — see docs/ARCHIVED_FEATURES.md.)
;
; A oneshot service is also a SINGLETON: systemd will not start a second run while
; one is still active. Under cron, an overrunning job could overlap the next tick
; and race itself on the CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION at the top of the SQL
; ("tuple concurrently updated"). That failure mode is now impossible by construction.
;
; Step 1 — debit inactive users (pure SQL, one transaction).
; `bash -c` is required for the `<` stdin redirect and `>>` append, which are shell
; syntax that systemd's ExecStart does not itself interpret.
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c '/usr/bin/docker exec -i cow-postgres psql -U cow_user -d cow_db < __REPO_DIR__/database/cron/expire-stale-streaks.sql >> __REPO_DIR__/logs/streak-expire.log 2>&1'

; Step 2 — Study Challenge expiry: lapse unaccepted invitations, close finished test
; windows (declaring the winner or a no-contest), and drop the generated study decks
; those closures leave behind. Pure SQL, one transaction, four idempotent passes.
; See docs/STUDY_CHALLENGE.md § 9 "The maintenance job".
;
; LAST ON PURPOSE, and independent of step 1. It shares nothing with it — different
; tables, no ordering constraint either way — so it goes last where a failure cannot
; stop the job that was here first. The converse also
; holds: because systemd aborts the unit when an earlier ExecStart fails, a broken
; step 1 means challenges are not expired this tick. That is acceptable (the passes
; are idempotent and the next hourly tick catches up) but it is worth knowing when
; reading a log that shows nothing happening.
;
; HOURLY IS THE REQUIRED GRANULARITY, not a convenience: every challenge boundary is
; 04:00 local, so exactly one timezone crosses one each hour.
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c '/usr/bin/docker exec -i cow-postgres psql -U cow_user -d cow_db < __REPO_DIR__/database/cron/expire-study-challenges.sql >> __REPO_DIR__/logs/study-challenges.log 2>&1'

; Each step appends to its own log file (unchanged from the cron setup, so the
; verification steps in the docs still apply). systemd additionally records each
; run's exit status in the journal: `journalctl --user -u cow-maintenance`.
